WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.364
Imagine an ai helper that can actually finish tasks instead of just chatting about them. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 209. You will also hear how an agent thinks through a job. A ranking from a full-day test, open ai is always on helper, and quick bits. The Washington Post just published a clear guide for people who have never thought about using an ai agent. An ai agent is a digital helper that takes a goal you give it and chops that goal into smaller steps. Then it uses tools like a web

00:00:30.364 --> 00:00:35.484
browser or an app to carry those steps out. Ask a really organized classmate to handle

00:00:35.484 --> 00:00:40.664
your whole weekend, and you will feel the idea. That classmate does not just toss suggestions

00:00:40.664 --> 00:00:46.124
at you. They grab the tickets, peek at the forecast, and ping you with reminders. Agents

00:00:46.124 --> 00:00:50.924
are moving toward handling pieces of your homework, your creative projects, and your daily errands.

00:00:51.344 --> 00:00:56.244
For students, that could mean organizing research for a paper. It could also mean pulling study

00:00:56.244 --> 00:00:57.904
notes together from different websites.

00:00:58.564 --> 00:01:03.284
For anyone curious about future jobs, learning to work with these helpers now is like learning

00:01:03.284 --> 00:01:05.184
a smartphone when those first arrived.

00:01:05.704 --> 00:01:10.104
You suddenly had a tiny computer in your pocket, and the people who got comfortable early

00:01:10.104 --> 00:01:10.964
had a head start.

00:01:11.524 --> 00:01:13.444
You do not need special skills to begin.

00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:19.024
Start with the same chatbots you already know, and just give them clearer, multi-step instructions.

00:01:20.045 --> 00:01:23.565
A chatbot is the familiar box that answers a question when you type.

00:01:23.565 --> 00:01:28.325
And agent is what happens when you give that box a whole job, not one question.

00:01:28.985 --> 00:01:33.025
Think of it like handing a friend a real shopping list instead of saying, go get food.

00:01:33.625 --> 00:01:39.225
You can try this right now on your phone or laptop with zero coding, open chat GPT or

00:01:39.225 --> 00:01:42.685
another chatbot called Claude, and type a request like this.

00:01:43.145 --> 00:01:47.845
Help me plan a simple study schedule for my next three tests, including short breaks and

00:01:47.845 --> 00:01:49.025
what to review each day.

00:01:49.025 --> 00:01:53.345
The more specific you are about what you want done, the better the agent works.

00:01:53.885 --> 00:01:57.805
These helpers can feel overwhelming at first because they seem to act on their own.

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:00.805
Start small with one clear goal at a time.

00:02:01.325 --> 00:02:05.605
It is like learning a single crafting recipe in Minecraft before you try to build a whole

00:02:05.605 --> 00:02:05.905
base.

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:11.085
Once you see an agent finish even a simple job like gathering links for a school project,

00:02:11.545 --> 00:02:13.205
the idea stops feeling mysterious.

00:02:13.725 --> 00:02:14.865
It starts feeling useful.

00:02:14.865 --> 00:02:20.045
That newspaper guide walks you through the basics even if the idea has never crossed your mind.

00:02:20.365 --> 00:02:24.985
The step-by-step approach is reassuring because you can experiment without breaking anything.

00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:30.145
Many readers who have never tried agents before will find that gentle on-ramp or relief.

00:02:30.825 --> 00:02:35.145
The real power comes from learning how to give instructions that match what you actually need.

00:02:35.785 --> 00:02:37.785
Do not expect the agent to read your mind.

00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:43.445
Over time, directing one of these helpers becomes as normal as searching the web or sending a text.

00:02:43.445 --> 00:02:47.921
An agent working through a job looks a lot like a video game quest.

00:02:48.361 --> 00:02:51.681
You give your character a mission and the game does not just stand there.

00:02:52.181 --> 00:02:56.961
It figures out a path, uses items from your inventory, and keeps going until the quest

00:02:56.961 --> 00:02:57.541
is complete.

00:02:58.241 --> 00:02:59.321
Now swap the pieces.

00:02:59.921 --> 00:03:04.081
The character is an AI and the game world is the internet plus the apps on your phone.

00:03:04.641 --> 00:03:06.201
The AI gets a goal from you.

00:03:06.641 --> 00:03:10.681
It decides which tools it needs, maybe searching the web or opening a calendar.

00:03:10.681 --> 00:03:14.301
It takes the first step, checks what happened, and keeps going.

00:03:14.841 --> 00:03:18.581
It continues until the goal is reached, or until it needs to ask you something.

00:03:19.041 --> 00:03:24.321
That loop of Decide, Act, Check, Repeat is what sets an agent apart from a regular chat

00:03:24.321 --> 00:03:24.581
bot.

00:03:24.981 --> 00:03:30.221
A regular chat bot answers one question and stops, like a search box waiting for your next tap.

00:03:30.921 --> 00:03:33.961
Earlier chat bots waited for every single instruction from you.

00:03:34.521 --> 00:03:37.041
Agents are built to keep working on their own for a while.

00:03:37.041 --> 00:03:41.781
fail. Seeing that way, the whole thing feels less like a spell and more like a very fast,

00:03:41.961 --> 00:03:46.941
very patient digital teammate. The process works because the agent keeps a running record

00:03:46.941 --> 00:03:52.101
of what it has already tried. That record is like a checklist you never lose, so it does

00:03:52.101 --> 00:03:57.501
not repeat mistakes. It also will not ask you the same question twice. It can switch between

00:03:57.501 --> 00:04:01.821
different tools without you having to tell it every time. You know how you bounce from a

00:04:01.821 --> 00:04:06.661
search to a notes app to a calendar without thinking about it? The agent can do that

00:04:06.661 --> 00:04:11.941
hop on its own. So the same helper can look up information, write a short summary, and

00:04:11.941 --> 00:04:17.181
then save that summary in a note. The important part is this. The agent only continues as

00:04:17.181 --> 00:04:22.501
long as it has a clear next step. If something goes wrong, or the goal becomes unclear, it

00:04:22.501 --> 00:04:27.221
pauses and waits for your input instead of guessing. That back and forth between action

00:04:27.221 --> 00:04:32.201
and checking results is what lets it handle longer jobs, like organizing study materials.

00:04:32.201 --> 00:04:37.021
The more you watch an agent work through its steps, the easier it becomes to see a teammate

00:04:37.021 --> 00:04:38.581
instead of another search box.

00:04:39.141 --> 00:04:41.901
If you can follow a quest in a game, you already have this.

00:04:42.942 --> 00:04:47.642
A writer at Wired spent a full day testing six different AI agents on ordinary tasks.

00:04:48.322 --> 00:04:50.842
Those tasks were writing, planning, and research.

00:04:51.402 --> 00:04:55.922
The goal was to see which one actually finishes what you ask, instead of just giving suggestions.

00:04:56.522 --> 00:05:01.062
That turns the confusing which AI should I use feeling into a ranking you can read in

00:05:01.062 --> 00:05:01.582
one sitting.

00:05:01.582 --> 00:05:05.902
Anything anyone who wants to try agents but does not know where to start should use that

00:05:05.902 --> 00:05:06.962
ranking as a doorway.

00:05:07.602 --> 00:05:11.422
Read which agent came out on top, then open that agent's app or site.

00:05:11.982 --> 00:05:14.442
Give it one small real task from your own life.

00:05:14.902 --> 00:05:20.322
Type this, make a grocery list for 3 meals this week using only ingredients under $5 each.

00:05:20.802 --> 00:05:23.982
Watch whether it actually builds the list or just talks about cooking.

00:05:24.602 --> 00:05:28.302
The ranking also shows how each agent handled the same tasks differently.

00:05:28.302 --> 00:05:32.162
Secondly, you can see exactly where one tool succeeded and another fell short.

00:05:32.662 --> 00:05:37.362
That side-by-side comparison helps beginners notice that not every agent works the same way,

00:05:37.802 --> 00:05:39.782
even when they look similar at first glance.

00:05:40.442 --> 00:05:45.062
After you try the top-ranked agent, test the second place one on the same grocery list,

00:05:45.442 --> 00:05:47.482
see if it feels better for your style of work.

00:05:47.942 --> 00:05:52.422
The best agent for one person might not be the best for everyone, which is why trying the

00:05:52.422 --> 00:05:54.562
winner on your own tasks is the next step.

00:05:54.562 --> 00:06:01.342
Open AI, the company behind chatGPT, turns some of its older coding tools into a new always

00:06:01.342 --> 00:06:02.542
on helper called dots.

00:06:03.142 --> 00:06:07.582
Dots runs in the background and can keep working on research or writing you started earlier.

00:06:08.082 --> 00:06:08.862
TechCrunch reports.

00:06:09.482 --> 00:06:12.342
Most chat bots forget everything the moment you close the tab.

00:06:12.782 --> 00:06:16.102
It is like a game that never saves, so you have to start the level over.

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:20.862
Dots is designed to feel more like a helper that sticks around and remembers where you left

00:06:20.862 --> 00:06:21.222
off.

00:06:21.222 --> 00:06:28.262
You can try it by going to the chat GPT website or app and looking for the dots feature if it is available in your region.

00:06:28.662 --> 00:06:31.642
Start by asking it to keep track of a simple on-going task.

00:06:32.322 --> 00:06:36.742
Try saying, help me brainstorm ideas for my next school project every day this week.

00:06:37.222 --> 00:06:40.042
Those older tools used to help people write computer code.

00:06:40.502 --> 00:06:44.262
Open AI turned them into something that stays active across different sessions.

00:06:44.782 --> 00:06:47.942
You do not have to repeat instructions every time you open the app.

00:06:47.942 --> 00:06:52.982
Because of that history, dots can handle structured tasks like keeping lists or tracking

00:06:52.982 --> 00:06:55.502
progress without forgetting what you were in the middle of.

00:06:56.002 --> 00:07:00.122
The executive behind the project has said he never wants to meet someone else's AI

00:07:00.122 --> 00:07:00.602
agent.

00:07:01.142 --> 00:07:04.562
A lot of care went into making this one feel reliable and personal.

00:07:05.222 --> 00:07:09.442
Once dots is running, you can check back later in the day and see what it has added to your

00:07:09.442 --> 00:07:09.882
project.

00:07:10.362 --> 00:07:13.082
You do not have to restart the conversation from scratch.

00:07:13.542 --> 00:07:17.042
That background memory stays with the work even after you close the app.

00:07:17.042 --> 00:07:22.044
People on Reddit are hunting for the most reliable free AI chat bot right now.

00:07:22.544 --> 00:07:28.904
A threat of honest experiences shows users switching between chat, GPT, and Gemini, which is Google's chat bot.

00:07:29.424 --> 00:07:34.184
They also try lesser-known options because free versions keep getting limited or less helpful.

00:07:34.824 --> 00:07:40.444
Readers share which free versions still work well for everyday questions, and which ones have become too restricted.

00:07:40.924 --> 00:07:45.864
That gives newcomers a practical starting point before they decide whether to try a paid plan later.

00:07:45.864 --> 00:07:47.304
That's it for today.

00:07:47.604 --> 00:07:51.124
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:07:51.584 --> 00:07:54.764
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:07:55.064 --> 00:07:56.644
That's literally how learning works.

00:07:57.364 --> 00:08:00.364
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

